Substituting the Pauli matrices gives $\sigma_{\hat n}
  =
  \begin{pmatrix}
    n_3 & n_1-in_2\\
    n_1+in_2 & -n_3
  \end{pmatrix}.$ It is convenient to abbreviate $n_-=n_1-in_2,
  \qquad
  n_+=n_1+in_2=n_-^*,$ so that $\sigma_{\hat n}
  =
  \begin{pmatrix}
    n_3&n_-\\
    n_+&-n_3
  \end{pmatrix}.$ The real diagonal and conjugate off-diagonal entries make the operator Hermitian.

