p.src:
The unnormalized vector has squared norm $\begin{aligned}
  \begin{pmatrix}
    1&\dfrac{1-n_3}{n_+}
  \end{pmatrix}
  \begin{pmatrix}
    1\\[3pt]\dfrac{1-n_3}{n_-}
  \end{pmatrix}
  &=
  1+\frac{(1-n_3)^2}{n_+n_-}
  \notag\\
  &=
  1+\frac{1-n_3}{1+n_3}
  =
  \frac{2}{1+n_3}.
  \end{aligned}$ Hence a normalized choice is $\ket{n,+}
  =
  \sqrt{\frac{1+n_3}{2}}
  \begin{pmatrix}
    1\\[5pt]\dfrac{1-n_3}{n_-}
  \end{pmatrix}.$ At $n_3=-1$, this coordinate expression is singular although the state is not; the eigenstate is simply $\ket d=(0,1)^T$. The singularity belongs to the phase convention, not to the spin.

